by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-17T15:26:12Z

We do want record and replay in HttpClient, so thanks for keeping at this. Let me be clearer about the direction than we have been so far, because the current shape needs a fair amount of rework and it is better you hear that now than after another round.

The main change: build on what is already there instead of alongside it. `MockHttpClient` plus `HarFileResponseFactory` already does the replay half, and it does it well: it matches on method, URL and body, it understands base64 bodies, `stream()` and `toStream()` work, and a miss throws. The genuinely new and useful part of your PR is the recording half. If the recorder writes files that `HarFileResponseFactory` can read, you get replay for free and there is only one HAR implementation in the component.

That also fixes a problem you may not have noticed. The current writer emits headers as `{"set-cookie": ["..."]}`, while HAR wants `[{"name": ..., "value": ...}]`, and it omits `time`, `timings`, `httpVersion`, `cookies`, `headersSize`, `bodySize` and `statusText`. Feeding one of your recordings to `HarFileResponseFactory` gives `Undefined array key "name"` and empty headers, and browser devtools cannot open the file either. Any fix to that format invalidates recordings people have already made, so it is worth settling before this ships rather than after.

Four other things that need to change before we can take it:

- Redaction on by default. Right now a recording contains the `Authorization` header, `Set-Cookie`, query string tokens and the request body verbatim, in a file that the workflow tells people to commit. It needs an allowlist or a filter list, enabled by default, with the option to widen it.
- A miss in replay mode must never reach the network. `REPLAY_AND_RECORD_IF_MISSING` is the default for `#[UseRecord]`, so renaming a URL in a test currently makes the suite call the real service. I would make throwing the default and require an explicit opt-in to record.
- `stream()` has to work. It currently fails with `AsyncResponse::stream(): Argument #1 ($responses) must be of type Traversable|array, NativeResponse given`, because `request()` does not wrap in `AsyncResponse`. This is what @stof raised in April and it is still the blocking one. Recording eagerly through `getContent()` also serialises concurrent requests, which changes what the tests under recording are measuring.
- Answering @welcoMattic on the process-wide state, since that question was for us: no, we should not keep `RecorderHttpClient::$mode` and `$record` as statics. With `framework.http_client.recorder` decorating every client, one stray `setMode()` changes behaviour everywhere, including in production. Please make it instance state.

Smaller ones for later: a class-level `#[UseRecord]` is silently ignored today because the subscriber calls `AttributeReader::forMethod()` rather than `forClassAndMethod()`, so the test quietly runs in passthrough; `Har/HarFile` uses `HttpKernel\Kernel::VERSION` and `FilesystemStore` uses `symfony/filesystem`, neither of which is declared; and the PhpUnit bridge references classes from HttpClient and HttpFoundation while requiring only `php`. Tests and CHANGELOG entries are missing throughout.

None of this is a rejection of the idea. It is a request to make the recorder produce real HAR that our existing reader consumes, to fail closed, and to keep secrets out of files people commit.

